      "text": "```python\ndef median(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the median of a nonempty list of numbers.\n    \n    For an odd-length list, the median is the middle element.\n    For an even-length list, the median is the average of the two middle elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must be nonempty\")\n    \n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_nums)\n    mid = n // 2\n    \n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return float(sorted_nums[mid])\n    else:\n        return (sorted_nums[mid - 1] + sorted_nums[mid]) / 2.0\n\n\n# Assert tests\nassert median([1, 3, 5]) == 3.0\nassert median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5\n```\n\n**Explanation (100 words):**\n\nFor an even-length list, the function first sorts the numbers. It calculates the middle index using integer division. Since there is no single middle element, it takes the two central elements at indices `mid - 1` and `mid`. These two values are summed and divided by 2.0 to compute their arithmetic mean, which is returned as the median. This approach ensures the median accurately represents the center of the data distribution for even-sized datasets, providing a value that lies between the two middle numbers.<|im_end|>",
