      "text": "```python\ndef median(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the median of a nonempty list of numbers.\n    \n    For an odd-length list, the median is the middle element.\n    For an even-length list, the median is the average of the two middle elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must be nonempty\")\n    \n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_nums)\n    mid = n // 2\n    \n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return float(sorted_nums[mid])\n    else:\n        return (sorted_nums[mid - 1] + sorted_nums[mid]) / 2.0\n\n\n# Assert tests\nassert median([1, 3, 5]) == 3.0\nassert median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5\n```\n\n**Explanation (100 words):**\n\nFor an even-length list, the function first sorts the numbers. It calculates the middle index as `n // 2`. Since there is no single middle element, it takes the two central elements at indices `mid - 1` and `mid`, adds them together, and divides by 2.0 to compute their arithmetic mean. This average becomes the median. For example, in `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the two middle values are 2 and 3, so the median is `(2 + 3) / 2 = 2.5`. This approach ensures the median accurately represents the center of the data distribution for even-sized datasets.<|im_end|>",
